//! check: run(exit=0, stdout="all: [] len 0\nagain: []")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: std.io, err.row, err.else
//!
//! `input_all` on EMPTY standard input: the loop's first read is `eof`, the
//! loop ends, and the answer is `""` — the end of input is what makes the
//! function return, so `eof` is not in its row at all.
//!
//! Empty input is the only path this rig can witness: `conform-run` has no
//! stdin injection (F-0046, unmoved for four sprints) and the runner wires
//! the null device, so the hit path — several lines joined with `"\n"` — is
//! proven in the toolchain's own tests and stated on the function rather
//! than asserted here. What this file DOES hold is the property that made
//! the function unwritable until this pin: the loop stops on one tag and
//! re-raises the others, so an empty stream is an empty answer and never a
//! miss.
//!
//! The second call proves the obvious thing worth proving anyway — the
//! function is not stateful and a second call at end-of-input is still `""`,
//! not a row.

use std.io

fn main() -> !int {
    let all = io.input_all() else "?"
    print("all: [{all}] len {all.len}")
    let again = io.input_all() else "?"
    print("again: [{again}]")
    0
}
